# Speckit Constitution

## Purpose
This constitution defines the core engineering principles for the project, centered on code quality, testing standards, user experience consistency, and performance requirements.

## Principles

### 1. Code Quality
- Write clean, readable code using consistent naming, spacing, and structure.
- Prefer explicit, self-documenting code over clever one-liners.
- Follow established language and framework conventions.
- Keep functions and components focused on a single responsibility.
- Review code through collaboration and constructive feedback before merging.

### 2. Testing Standards
- Every feature must have automated tests covering expected behavior and edge cases.
- Maintain a high level of test coverage for critical logic and user-facing flows.
- Use unit tests for small units, integration tests for component interactions, and end-to-end tests for user journeys.
- Run tests locally before commits and ensure tests pass in continuous integration.
- Treat failing tests as blockers, not optional warnings.

### 3. User Experience Consistency
- Follow a consistent visual and interaction design across the product.
- Use shared components, spacing, and typography patterns to maintain UI harmony.
- Prioritize accessibility and usability for all users.
- Ensure behavior is predictable and responsive across devices.
- Document design decisions and keep the experience coherent as the product grows.

### 4. Performance Requirements
- Optimize for fast load times and smooth runtime behavior.
- Avoid unnecessary work in render paths, network requests, and data processing.
- Measure performance with metrics, not assumptions.
- Keep resource usage efficient and respect device constraints.
- Continuously identify and address bottlenecks as part of regular development.

## Commitment
We commit to applying these principles in every release, balancing speed with responsibility, and improving quality continuously through discipline and collaboration.
